#{[17592186216646 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216648] [17592186216651 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216653] [17592186216653 :answer/value "\\exp (-\\int h(y)dy)"] [17592186216654 :answer/value "\\exp (\\int h(y)dy)"] [17592186217762 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=2xy-x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$"] [17592186217762 :question/worked-solution "For $P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy=0$, an integrating factor $I(x)$ must satisfy\n$$\nI P_y=I'Q+I Q_x,\\qquad \\frac{I'}I=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=g(x).\n$$\nConsequently\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\mathrm dx\\right).\n$$\nFor this equation take $P=x-2xy$, $Q=1$. Then $g(x)=-2x$ and $I(x)=e^{-x^2}$; direct differentiation verifies exactness. The ratio criterion is used where its denominator is nonzero."] [17592186217793 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-({x}^{3}+2{y}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant.\n\nUse the displayed quotient only where it is defined, and use the integrating-factor method on regions with $y\\ne0$."] [17592186217793 :question/worked-solution "For an integrating factor depending only on $y$, use\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}\\,\\mathrm dy\\right).\n$$\nThe displayed quotient is evaluated where $P\\ne0$. Since it simplifies to $4/y$, we can choose\n\n$$\nI(y)=e^{-4\\ln|y|}=\\frac1{y^4},\\qquad y\\ne0.\n$$\nMultiplication gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{y^3}\\,\\mathrm dx+\n\\left(-\\frac{x^3}{y^4}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm dy=0.\n$$\nThis equation is exact, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\frac{x^2}{y^3}\\right)\n=-\\frac{3x^2}{y^4}\n=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(-\\frac{x^3}{y^4}-2\\right).\n$$\nTo find a potential $F(x,y)$, integrate its $x$-derivative while holding $y$ fixed:\n\n$$\nF_x=\\frac{x^2}{y^3}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x^3}{3y^3}+G(y).\n$$\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the other coefficient:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x^3}{y^4}+G'(y)=-\\frac{x^3}{y^4}-2,\n\\qquad G'(y)=-2.\n$$\nTherefore the requested implicit general solution is\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{x^3}{3y^3}-2y=c},\\qquad y\\ne0.\n$$\nIndeed, $\\mathrm dF=y^{-4}(P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy)$, so the level curves give the original differential equation on the stated regions. Although the quotient formula initially requires $P\\ne0$, the resulting integrating factor and exact equation extend to $x=0$ whenever $y\\ne0$.\n\nThe original equation also admits $y=0$. That solution lies outside the question's explicit $y\\ne0$ region, where the integrating factor is undefined. The factor $1/3$ in the potential is necessary: omitting it changes $F_x$ and does not merely rename the arbitrary constant."] [17592186217795 :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)={e}^{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that the function $I(y)={e}^{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$"] [17592186217795 :question/worked-solution "Multiply each coefficient of the original equation by $I(y)=e^y$. The equation to test is\n$$\n((y+x)e^y)\\,\\mathrm dx+(xe^y)\\,\\mathrm dy=0.\n$$\nThis is the required transformed equation. In fact,\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}((y+x)e^y)=(1+x+y)e^y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(xe^y)=e^y.\n$$\nThese are not identically equal, so $e^y$ does **not** make this equation exact. Selecting the product equation alone does not establish that it is an integrating factor."] [17592186217796 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n(7x-4xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy-7x),\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$"] [17592186217796 :question/worked-solution "For an integrating factor depending only on $x$, exactness requires\n$$\n\\frac{I'}I=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=g(x),\\qquad I(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\mathrm dx\\right).\n$$\nHere $P=7x-4xy$ and $Q=1$, so\n$$\nP_y=-4x,\\qquad Q_x=0.\n$$\nThus $g(x)=-4x$ and $I(x)=e^{-2x^2}$. These identify the four requested selections in their existing fields."] [17592186217797 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=-x({y}^{2}+1)\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$"] [17592186217797 :question/worked-solution "For an integrating factor depending only on $y$, exactness requires\n$$\nI P_y+I'P=I Q_x,\\qquad\\frac{I'}I=-\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}=-h(y).\n$$\nTherefore the requested selections are\n$$\nh(y)=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P},\\qquad I(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm dy\\right).\n$$\nHere $P=x(y^2+1)$, $Q=1$, giving $h(y)=2y/(1+y^2)$ where the ratio is defined. Hence $I(y)=1/(1+y^2)$; this factor extends to every real $y$ and makes the equation exact even at $x=0$."] [17592186228333 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186228337] [17592186228345 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186228346] [17592186228567 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186228571] [17592186229626 :question/worked-solution "Try $y_p=Be^{2x}$, so $y_p'=2Be^{2x}$. Substitution gives\n$$\n9(2Be^{2x})+18Be^{2x}=4e^{2x},\\qquad36B=4.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\frac19e^{2x}.\n$$\nDirect substitution verifies the equation; the complementary term is not included in this particular solution."] [17592186229628 :question/worked-solution "Try $y_p=Be^{2x}$. Substitution into $y'-6y=8e^{2x}$ gives $(2-6)B=8$, so $B=-2$. Adding the complementary solution,\n$$\ny=Ae^{6x}-2e^{2x}.\n$$\nThe initial condition gives $1=A-2$, so $A=3$ and\n$$\ny(x)=3e^{6x}-2e^{2x}.\n$$\nIts residual $y'-6y-8e^{2x}$ is zero and $y(0)=1$."] [17592186229635 :question/worked-solution "The forcing term has the same exponential form as the complementary solution, so try $y_p=Bxe^{4x}$. Then\n$$\ny_p'=Be^{4x}+4Bxe^{4x},\\qquad y_p'-4y_p=Be^{4x}.\n$$\nThus $B=3$. Adding the complementary solution gives\n$$\ny(x)=(A+3x)e^{4x},\\qquad A\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\nThis family satisfies $y'-4y=3e^{4x}$."] [17592186229641 :question/worked-solution "Try $y_p=Be^{-3x}$, so $y_p'=-3Be^{-3x}$. Substituting gives\n$$\n5(-3Be^{-3x})+10Be^{-3x}=15e^{-3x},\\qquad-5B=15.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny_p(x)=-3e^{-3x}.\n$$\nDirect substitution verifies the equation."] [17592186229649 :question/worked-solution "Because $e^{3x}$ already occurs in the complementary solution, try $y_p=Bxe^{3x}$. Substitution gives\n$$\ny_p'-3y_p=Be^{3x}=9e^{3x},\n$$\nso $B=9$. The general solution is $y=(A+9x)e^{3x}$. The condition $y(0)=2$ gives $A=2$, hence\n$$\ny(x)=(2+9x)e^{3x}.\n$$\nBoth the differential equation and initial condition are satisfied."] [17592186229692 :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=0$ into the stated model $Q(t)=\\alpha+\\beta e^{-kt}$. Since $e^0=1$ and the initial salt mass is $6\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, $\\alpha+\\beta=Q(0)=6$."] [17592186229694 :question/worked-solution "The volume is $V(t)=120+(9-6)t=120+3t$. Overflow begins when $V=210$, so $t=30\\,\\mathrm{min}$. Mass balance gives $Q′=4.5-6Q/(120+3t)$. Its solution satisfying $Q(0)=30$ is $Q=\\frac12(120+3t)-30(120/(120+3t))^2$. At $t=30$, $Q=95.204\\ldots\\,\\mathrm{lb}$, hence $95.2\\,\\mathrm{lb}$."] [17592186229725 :question/worked-solution "The volume stays $900\\,\\mathrm{L}$. The salt inflow is $0.02(30)=0.6\\,\\mathrm{kg/min}$ and outflow is $30Q/900=Q/30$. Thus $Q′+Q/30=0.6$. With $Q(0)=10$, $Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}$. Consequently $Q(6)=11.4501\\ldots\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, or $11.45\\,\\mathrm{kg}$."] [17592186235391 :question/problem "$$\n\\int y^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\ln({{field-1}})+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186235444 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235482 :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"> $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\hspace{0.167em}-$ </td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">  {{field-1}} </td> </tr> <tr> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"> ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}(x)}$ </td> </tr> </tbody></table></div>"] [17592186235523 :question/problem "Find the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}"] [17592186235523 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
